# !! DO NOT TYPE A REAL VALUE INTO THIS FILE. It is committed and this repository is
# !! PUBLIC. The placeholders below are deliberately not blank, so that a value left
# !! unreplaced fails loudly at authentication instead of silently becoming an empty
# !! credential. Copy this file first; edit only the copy.
#
# TEMPLATE — committed. Copy it, do not edit it in place:
#
#   cp env.secret.stalwart-automation.example .env.secret.stalwart-automation
#   $EDITOR .env.secret.stalwart-automation
#   ../../../scripts/encrypt-env-files.sh apps/production/email-stalwart
#
# Run the encrypt step YOURSELF. The agent must never create or read the real file.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS IS FOR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The `automation` principal, used by the stalwart-sieve-reconcile CronJob to keep every
# account's active Sieve script matching git.
#
# This is the one job that cannot use an API key. Per-account Sieve scripts are not in the
# configuration registry — the Account object has no script field — so the only way to set
# one is to authenticate *as* the user. ManageSieve accepts a composite login
# `<target>%<impersonator>`, which is what `impersonate` exists for. API keys are refused by
# mail protocols, so a password is unavoidable here.
#
# STALWART_USER is the impersonator's full address, not a bare name: the job builds
# `<target-address>%<STALWART_USER>` for each account.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECURITY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This credential can read and rewrite any account's Sieve scripts, and `impersonate` is the
# strongest permission on the server. It is deliberately NOT the admin account: upstream's
# security guidance is that admin accounts must never be used to reach IMAP, JMAP or WebDAV,
# and this is exactly that kind of access.
#
# `automation` holds `replace` permissions — only these, and no others:
#
#   impersonate  authenticate
#   sysAccountQuery  sysAccountGet                 (list accounts to reconcile)
#   sieveAuthenticate  sieveListScripts  sieveGetScript  sievePutScript  sieveSetActive
#
# Note the omissions. No sieveDeleteScript, so it cannot destroy a user's filters. No
# sysAccountUpdate/Create/Destroy and no sysAccountPassword*, so it cannot change accounts or
# credentials. The reconciler additionally refuses to overwrite any active script that is not
# the one it manages — but the permission set means that is defence in depth, not the only
# thing standing in the way.
#
# Rotation: change the password in the admin UI, copy this template again, paste, re-encrypt.
# Nothing caches it; the next scheduled run picks it up.
#
# No quotes, no trailing spaces — everything after `=` is the value.

STALWART_USER=automation@willeke.com
STALWART_PASSWORD=<REPLACE-ME>
